WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.760
Four men had sperm that could not swim.

2
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.140
Three of them also could not clear mucus from their airways.

3
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:17.700
Under the electron microscope, their cilia lacked small projections called dynein

4
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.780
arms. One missing part caused two failures in two organs.

5
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.960
A sperm tail and an airway cilium must share a machine.

6
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:34.033
Sperm tails and airway cilia are built on the same axoneme.

7
00:00:34.753 --> 00:00:37.013
It is a cylinder of microtubules.

8
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:42.333
In the classic pattern, nine outer doublets surround a central pair.

9
00:00:43.373 --> 00:00:48.893
Each doublet is a complete A-tubule with an incomplete B-tubule fused to it.

10
00:00:49.553 --> 00:00:53.913
The whole cylinder grows from a basal body, which anchors it in the cell.

11
00:00:54.783 --> 00:01:01.363
Along each A-tubule sit two rows of dynein arms, the outer and inner arms.

12
00:01:02.223 --> 00:01:04.403
These are the arms that the four men lacked.

13
00:01:05.443 --> 00:01:09.303
Each motor head acts on the B-tubule of the next doublet.

14
00:01:09.883 --> 00:01:13.303
It walks toward that tubule's minus end, at the base.

15
00:01:14.163 --> 00:01:17.043
So each doublet pushes its neighbour toward the tip.

16
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:20.277
Why does pushing make a bend?

17
00:01:21.337 --> 00:01:26.017
Think of two neighbouring doublets as two strips of card, side by side.

18
00:01:26.637 --> 00:01:30.817
They are glued together at one end and tied loosely along their length.

19
00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:33.817
Push one strip toward the far end.

20
00:01:34.457 --> 00:01:37.197
The pair cannot slide apart, so it curves.

21
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.620
In the axoneme, the base is anchored and the links resist.

22
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.240
So shear between doublets becomes a bend.

23
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.920
Summers and Gibbons took the resistance away.

24
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.860
They digested sea-urchin sperm axonemes briefly with trypsin, and added ATP.

25
00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.780
Seen by dark-field microscopy, the doublets slid apart.

26
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.120
Dynein does not shorten microtubules.

27
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:05.600
It slides them.

28
00:02:06.283 --> 00:02:09.603
This gives a test for a paralysed flagellum.

29
00:02:10.423 --> 00:02:14.383
Isolate the axonemes, cut the links, and add ATP.

30
00:02:15.403 --> 00:02:20.983
If the doublets slide apart, at least some motors work, so check that both rows of

31
00:02:20.983 --> 00:02:26.603
arms are present. If they are, the fault probably lies in regulating them, or in

32
00:02:26.603 --> 00:02:28.183
turning sliding into bending.

33
00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:34.723
The radial spokes, the central pair, and the nexin-dynein regulatory complex are the

34
00:02:34.723 --> 00:02:40.403
places to look. If they do not slide, suspect missing or inactive dynein arms.

35
00:02:41.243 --> 00:02:43.803
A bend is sliding that the links resist.
